WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:06.828
Liquid Hand Wash and Dish Wash by Percentage Formula. This is module 2 of the course.

2
00:00:07.199 --> 00:00:14.570
Today you'll mix a mild hand wash in the bucket, thicken it without spoiling it, and choose a dish liquid by real plate counts.

3
00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:19.906
Everything you see going onto this scale is weighed as a percentage of the batch.

4
00:00:20.231 --> 00:00:27.004
That way the same sheet works for a 2 kg test or a 200 kg batch. You just multiply.

5
00:00:27.362 --> 00:00:34.706
This is a starting formula for test batches. Active matter comes to 9.9%, a moderate level for hands.

6
00:00:35.706 --> 00:00:46.948
Chiamaka in Enugu wanted 10 kg, about 20 bottles of 500 ml. She multiplied each percentage by 10,000 g and divided by 100.

7
00:00:47.285 --> 00:00:53.343
Then she added every line to check it came to exactly 10,000 g, and ticked each weight as it went in.

8
00:00:54.343 --> 00:01:01.223
Gloves and goggles on before you open the SLES paste or fragrance oil. Both can irritate skin and eyes.

9
00:01:02.223 --> 00:01:09.398
Here's the clean drum. Weigh in the water, keep back about 10% for later, and stir in the EDTA until it dissolves.

10
00:01:09.814 --> 00:01:16.123
Now the SLES paste goes in, in pieces, while the paddle moves slowly. Stir gently so you don't whip in air.

11
00:01:16.453 --> 00:01:20.934
When it's smooth with no lumps, add the CAPB and glycerin and stir until even.

12
00:01:21.934 --> 00:01:27.732
Watch the dropper. Premix the fragrance with a little CAPB first, so the hand wash doesn't turn cloudy.

13
00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:30.254
Then add the preservative and any colour.

14
00:01:31.254 --> 00:01:37.321
Let it rest for 15 minutes, then dip a strip. You're aiming for about 5.5 to 6.5.

15
00:01:37.786 --> 00:01:44.555
If it's high, bring it down with citric acid solution, sample first, exactly as you learned in module 1.

16
00:01:45.555 --> 00:01:50.454
Salt thickens up to a peak, then the liquid goes thin again, and you can't take salt out.

17
00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:56.596
So Ruth in Jos weighed four 200 g samples and salted each one differently.

18
00:01:57.094 --> 00:02:08.794
Thickness rose to 1.5% and fell at 2.0%. Her peak was around 1.5%, which fit her 15 to 25 second specification.

19
00:02:09.794 --> 00:02:19.609
Now watch it thicken. Ruth dissolved her 300 g of salt in 1,200 g of kept-back water to make a 20% brine.

20
00:02:19.971 --> 00:02:27.367
She poured it in three portions, stirring and testing after each. The batch reached about 20 seconds without passing the peak.

21
00:02:28.367 --> 00:02:35.892
Rest the batch covered overnight so bubbles rise. Then check it against your specification and your standard sample, and fill.

22
00:02:36.371 --> 00:02:41.239
A batch that fails is held back and corrected, never sold because it's almost fine.

23
00:02:42.239 --> 00:02:54.454
Dish liquid has a harder job. In her notebook, Zainab in Kano tested two formulas the same way: 2 g of oil on each plate, 5 litres of warm water, a 5 ml dose.

24
00:02:54.834 --> 00:03:04.067
She washed until the foam disappeared and counted plates. Formula A cost more per litre but less per plate, and beat the market brand. She chose A.

25
00:03:05.067 --> 00:03:10.868
Test the dish liquid too. Keep it around 6 to 8, gentle on hands that soak for a long time.

26
00:03:11.295 --> 00:03:18.379
Never add caustic soda or soda ash for extra power. It irritates skin and can dull aluminium pots.

27
00:03:19.379 --> 00:03:23.541
Look at these bottles on the stall: proper detergent bottles with a label.

28
00:03:23.924 --> 00:03:31.902
Never use old soft-drink or water bottles. Someone may drink from it. Print "Keep out of reach of children" and the first aid line.

29
00:03:32.902 --> 00:03:38.597
Cosmetic dyes are very strong, so make a 1% stock solution and dose that instead.

30
00:03:39.012 --> 00:03:43.581
When something goes wrong, change only one thing per test sample and write it down.

31
00:03:44.581 --> 00:03:53.657
Now go through the lessons below. Make your 2 kg test batch, run your own plate test, and draw your salt curve. Your numbers will tell you what works.

